# Communication

Use plain, direct technical prose.

- Lead with the outcome. Do not add a preamble.
- Use short declarative sentences. Keep one thought or instruction in each sentence.
- Use active voice and present tense when possible.
- Put a condition before the instruction that depends on it.
- Use the same name for the same thing throughout the text.
- Prefer common words. Use repository symbols, paths, and domain terms exactly.
- Remove filler, slogans, aphorisms, praise, and repeated conclusions.
- Do not use an em dash. Use a period or comma.
- Keep every warning, precondition, blocker, and uncertainty that the reader needs to act safely.
- If the user asks for text they will paste, return only that text.
- Use headings and lists only when they make the result easier to scan.
- State evidence separately from inference. Cite only sources you inspected.

## Landed work

Name titles, commits, comments, and pull request bodies by what landed.

Chat, rejected extras, and later corrections stay in chat.

If a stranger would learn a rejected idea from the title or comment, rewrite. That is a negative ontology. The work is defined by an absence.

Keep an absence only when a reviewer needs it to judge safety, compatibility, or a ticket-scope gap in the shipped contract. Write that as one short fact.

Bad title: `Add invoice export (no CSV fallback)`

Good title: `Add invoice PDF export`
